    r"""Inside the rod, a coaxial Gaussian cylinder gives $$E\,(2\pi r l) = \frac{\rho\,\pi r^2 l}{\varepsilon_0} \;\Rightarrow\; E = \frac{\rho r}{2\varepsilon_0}.$$ $$|V_A - V_B| = \int_{0.02}^{0.04}\frac{\rho r}{2\varepsilon_0}\,dr = \frac{\rho}{4\varepsilon_0}\left(0.04^2 - 0.02^2\right) = \frac{50\times10^{-9}}{4(8.85\times10^{-12})}(1.2\times10^{-3}) = 1.7\ \text{V}.$$ **[NOTE]** The official solution writes $E = \rho r/\varepsilon_0$ (missing the 2), but its final formula $\rho(b^2 - a^2)/4\varepsilon_0$ and its answer use the correct $\rho r/2\varepsilon_0$. **Why this method:** Gauss' law gives $E(r)$ from symmetry, then $\Delta V = -\int E\,dr$.""",
